  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "\"We\" is a first-person plural subject pronoun referring to the speaker and at least one other person. It represents a group including \"I\" (inclusive of the listener) or \"I\" and others (exclusive of the listener). Examples include \"We live here\" (daily life) or \"We ordered pizza\" (shared action), with synonyms including \"us\" and \"ourselves\". Usage Examples of \"We\" Subject of a sentence: \"We are going to the park,\" \"We hope you can come\". Including the audience: \"We need to work together to solve this\". Royal/Impersonal \"We\": Used by royalty or authors to represent themselves, e.g., \"We are not amused\". Before a noun: \"We students,\" \"We teachers\". Reflexive usage: \"We did it ourselves\". Synonyms and Related Terms Us (as a pronoun/object): \"They saw us\". Ourselves (reflexive): \"We saw ourselves in the mirror\". Our (possessive adjective): \"We finished our work\". We're (contraction): \"We are\". Contextual Notes Inclusive vs. Exclusive: \"We\" can include the listener (\"We should go,\" meaning you and I) or exclude them (\"We are leaving,\" meaning my group, not you). Common mistake: Confusing \"we\" (subject) with \"us\" (object). Use \"we\" when the group is doing the action.",
